================================================================================
What this repository is
================================================================================
Beluga is Infrastructure-as-Code: Vagrantfile, Helm charts, Kubernetes
manifests, and bootstrap/demo scripts that declaratively provision a
self-hosted data platform. This repository does NOT compile, vendor, or
redistribute any third-party binary or container image. Every component
listed below is pulled at deploy time from its own upstream registry, under
its own upstream license — this repo only references it by name and version.

================================================================================
Third-party components this platform deploys
================================================================================
The full, versioned inventory — one row per component, each with its own
License column — is the Single Source of Truth at VERSIONS.md, not
duplicated here. The large majority are Apache License 2.0 (Kubernetes,
Cilium, ArgoCD, Trino, Airflow, Superset, Keycloak, OPA, OpenFGA,
OpenMetadata, OpenSearch, APISIX, etcd, and others). Exceptions worth
naming directly:

- OpenLDAP server binary — OpenLDAP Public License, Version 2.8 (the
  `vegardit/openldap` image wrapper itself is Apache-2.0; the OpenLDAP
  software it runs is not). Verbatim text: see the upstream project at
  https://www.openldap.org/software/release/license.html
- PostgreSQL (run via the CloudNativePG operator, itself Apache-2.0) —
  the PostgreSQL License, a permissive MIT-style license.
- curl (bootstrap/registration Job image) — the curl license, a permissive
  MIT/X-derivative license.
- Python — Python Software Foundation License 2.0.

On copyleft: Beluga links no third-party code into anything it ships. It
deploys each component as its own container and reaches it over a network
protocol, so a copyleft component deployed this way carries no obligation
beyond attribution — the boundary is a separate process, not a derived
work. GPL and LGPL components are therefore acceptable in that position.
AGPL is the exception to check for, since network use alone triggers its
source-offer obligation; nothing in VERSIONS.md is currently AGPL.

The constraint that does apply is in-process linking: a library imported
directly into code this repository distributes. The demo Postgres client
`psycopg2-binary` (LGPL-3.0-or-later) was that shape, and was deleted —
though as dead code no manifest executed, not for its license. A Postgres
driver reintroduced into this repository's own scripts should be
permissively licensed, such as `pg8000` (BSD-3-Clause).

Either way this covers only what Beluga declares; an upstream image may
bundle components of its own, which the SBOM below reports.

For an SBOM of what is actually deployed on a running cluster, generate one
from live state rather than trusting this file — see
`scripts/generate-sbom.sh`.

